<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hadrian's Villa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 118–138</span> · Also known as Villa Adriana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hadrian's Villa is the vast country residence built by the Roman emperor Hadrian near Tibur, modern Tivoli, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, between about 118 and 138. Covering more than 100 hectares, it combined palaces, baths, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>, libraries and pools inspired by buildings the emperor had seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, such as the Canopus. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hadrians-villa.jpg" alt="Hadrian's Villa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FrDr · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Villa_Hadriana_(Villa_Adriana_Tivoli)_1000_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
